        <p class="profile-detail-region">Movement · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">May Fourth Movement</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, where it began at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiananmen">Tiananmen</a> and in the universities; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, where the strikes of June 1919 moved its centre; the larger cities across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> among them; Chinese students in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>; echoes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penang">Penang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> in British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">The New Culture Movement, 1915–1921, its broader setting; the demonstration of May 4, 1919 and the student strikes of May; the June 3 arrests and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> general strike, ended on June 12, 1919; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>’s refusal to sign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>; the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> in 1921.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cai-yuanpei">Cai Yuanpei</a>, principal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peking-university">Peking University</a>, who resigned in protest; Chen Duxiu and Li Dazhao, leaders of the movement and founders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hu-shih">Hu Shih</a>, advocate of the vernacular; Chen Hengzhe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-xun">Lu Xun</a>, writers of early vernacular fiction; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai">Zhou Enlai</a> and Chen Tanqiu; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> members such as Luo Jialun.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The dismissal of Cao Rulin, Zhang Zongxiang and Lu Zongyu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>’s refusal to sign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>, a largely symbolic victory, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> for the moment; the rise of written vernacular Chinese and modern Chinese literature, and of Marxism and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>.</span></div>
        </div>
