    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the May Fourth Movement</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai.jpg" alt="Yuan Shikai" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Yuan Shikai <span class="kid-tale-cr">Photo by Rio V. De Sieux [1], Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>More than 100 years ago, after President Yuan Shikai died, warlords ruled much of China.</p><p>The government in Beijing could do little against powerful foreign countries.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles.jpg" alt="Treaty of Versailles" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Treaty of Versailles <span class="kid-tale-cr">David Lloyd George, Woodrow Wilson and Georges Clemenceau, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>After the First World War, a peace treaty gave Germany's rights in Shandong, a part of China, to Japan.</p><p>Many in China felt betrayed, because China had sent 140,000 workers to help the Allies in the war.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tiananmen.jpg" alt="Tiananmen" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Tiananmen <span class="kid-tale-cr">Balon Greyjoy, CC0</span></figcaption></figure><p>On the fourth of May, over 4,000 students gathered in front of Tiananmen in Beijing to protest.</p><p>When nearly 1,000 students were arrested, workers and shopkeepers stopped work to support them.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fourteen-points.jpg" alt="Fourteen Points" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Fourteen Points <span class="kid-tale-cr">William Orpen, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>The government sacked three officials, and China refused to sign the treaty, though Japan kept Shandong for a while.</p><p>Many Chinese stopped trusting Western promises, such as President Wilson's Fourteen Points.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lu-xun.jpg" alt="Lu Xun" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Lu Xun <span class="kid-tale-cr">Unknown author, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Writers began to write the way people spoke, instead of in the old formal Chinese.</p><p>Lu Xun and Chen Hengzhe wrote some of the first stories in everyday Chinese.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mao-zedong.jpg" alt="Mao Zedong" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Mao Zedong <span class="kid-tale-cr">Chen Zhengqing (1917–1966), Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Two of its leaders, Chen Duxiu and Li Dazhao, helped found the Chinese Communist Party.</p><p>Today the fourth of May is celebrated as Youth Day in China.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>On May 4, 1919, over 4,000 students marched to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiananmen">Tiananmen</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>’ award of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>’s rights in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> government’s weak response.</li><li>When the government arrested nearly 1,000 students in June, workers and merchants struck in their support, the movement’s centre moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, and the government dismissed three officials accused of collaborating with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</li><li>A turning point of the New Culture Movement, which called for “Mr. Science” and “Mr. Democracy” in place of “Mr. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>”, it made written vernacular Chinese the language of modern literature and helped give rise to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1917 Chen Hengzhe published the short story One Day in an overseas student quarterly, a year before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-xun">Lu Xun</a>’s Diary of a Madman, which has often been wrongly credited as the first vernacular Chinese fiction.</p></aside>
    </section>

